I would like to know when the destructor for thrown object is called.  I
expected that it is called only one time (and not two or three times)
after the catch block is executed.  So in order to check that, I wrote
a simple program and it gave me three different outputs when compiled
with Sun CC, DEC cxx and GNU g++ (last gave me internal compiler
error, but when I commented last line of main() it compiled OK).

Now, Am I doing something totaly wrong, or that three compilers
generate incorect code (and which one generated correct code, if any)?

Here is program, and the outputs it gives when compiled with before
mentioned compilers:

#include <iostream.h>

class exp {
public:
  exp(int x);
  ~exp();
  int i;
};

exp::exp(int x) {
   cout << "Constructor exp(" << x << ")\n";
   i = x;
}

exp::~exp() {
   cout << "Destructor ~exp()\n";
}

main() {
  try {
    cout << "Throwing exception\n";
    throw exp(1);
  } catch (exp a) {
    cout << "Catched exp(" << a.i << ")\n";
  }
  cout << "Exiting\n";
}

When compiled with Sun CC:
Throwing exception
Constructor exp(1)
Catched exp(1)
Destructor ~exp()
Destructor ~exp()
Exiting

When compiled with DEC cxx:
Throwing exception
Constructor exp(1)
Destructor ~exp()
Catched exp(1)
Destructor ~exp()
Destructor ~exp()
Exiting

When compiled with GNU g++ (last line of main() commented):
Throwing exception
Constructor exp(1)
Destructor ~exp()
Catched exp(1)

