A,

There are quite a few things going on here.
1. You are catching you exception class by value. This makes 2 copys of the
object thrown.
2. You do not see this as you did not override the copy ctor as well.
3. g++ doesn't implement exception handling well, so don't use it for them.
4. Because of #1, the most correct is the DEC solution.

When you pass an exception object by value, the object is copied to a
temporary because the object you explicitly created goes out of scope when
the throw is executed. Then when you catch the temporary, it must be copied
into your local variable (in this case, 'a'). Thus you get 3 constructors
(2 copy and one explicit) and 3 destructors. You should change your catch
to use 'a' by reference. This way it is only copied once (you cannot get
around the throw copy).

Just as an aside, use "\n" in your cout calls does NOT flush the output
stream so you may not always get what you expect. You should use the endl
manipulator :
cout << "Hello World" << endl; 	// Executes a new line and flushes the
stream.

Good Luck, 
Gene Thompson

PS. For a good discussion on exceptions, see "More Effective C++" by Scott
Meyers.


